## 1. Route handler

Create `app/api/auth/[...all]/route.ts`:

```ts
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";
import { toNextJsHandler } from "better-auth/next-js";

export const { GET, POST } = toNextJsHandler(auth);
```

**Version note:** This App Router setup applies to all Next.js versions. For the `pages` router, use `toNodeHandler` with `bodyParser` disabled instead.

## 2. proxy.ts (session validation)

Create `proxy.ts`:

```ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { headers } from "next/headers";
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";

export async function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
    const session = await auth.api.getSession({
        headers: await headers()
    })

    if(!session) {
        return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/sign-in", request.url));
    }

    return NextResponse.next();
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/dashboard"],
};
```

**Version note:** Use this file in Next.js 16+, where "middleware" is renamed "proxy." In Next.js 15.2.0+, the same logic goes in `middleware.ts` with `runtime: "nodejs"` in the config. That runtime is experimental before Next.js 16. In Next.js 13 through 15.1.x, middleware runs on the Edge Runtime and can't make these database-backed calls, so use the cookie-based check instead.

## 3. nextCookies plugin

```ts
import { betterAuth } from "better-auth";
import { nextCookies } from "better-auth/next-js";

export const auth = betterAuth({
    //...your config
    plugins: [nextCookies()] // make sure this is the last plugin in the array
})
```

**Version note:** No version-specific details are given. Without this plugin, cookies set by calls like `signInEmail` inside server actions aren't applied. The plugin should be the last entry in `plugins`.

## 4. Server-side getSession in a server component

```tsx
import { auth } from "@/lib/auth";
import { headers } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export default async function DashboardPage() {
    const session = await auth.api.getSession({
        headers: await headers()
    })

    if(!session) {
        redirect("/sign-in")
    }

    return <h1>Welcome {session.user.name}</h1>
}
```

**Version note:** `headers()` is awaited in this example, matching the Next.js 15+ async API. Server components can't set cookies, so the cookie cache isn't refreshed from this context. It refreshes when the client interacts with the server through server actions or route handlers.

